# 2026-08-29 — 363 -> 361. TWO rows, and the fix is ONE CALL that already
# existed on the sibling path. A compound assignment `place op= e` asked
# `place_write_supported` — "can the address machinery lower this" — and never
# `check_place_writable`, which `lower_place_assign` calls on every plain
# `place = e`. So the two spellings disagreed one token apart:
#   let rofl: &V = &mut test;  rofl.n = 1i64;   -> REFUSED
#   let rofl: &V = &mut test;  rofl.n += 1i64;  -> ADMITTED
# i.e. the checker admitted a WRITE THROUGH A SHARED REFERENCE whenever the
# write wore an `op=`. Closed, with the diagnostics pinned in fail fixtures:
#   issue-85765  E0596/E0594  "assignment through a shared reference (variable 'rofl' is `&`)"
#   issue-93093  E0594        "assignment through a shared reference (variable 'self' is `&`)"
# PRICED BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN (scripts/probe-batch.sh, probe `opeqwritable`,
# 2026-08-29): 15 fires over the 363-row ledger, CEILING 2, COST 0. Predicted
# those two rows BY NAME; closed exactly those two, diff empty both ways.
# ⚠ CEILING 2 IS BELOW THIS ROUND'S OWN FUNDING BAR OF 3, and it was funded
# anyway: the two mechanisms that cleared the bar numerically (`callrootref`,
# `callfldw`, both ceiling 4 / cost 0) each refuse a legal program — `match *x
# { Cycle::Node(ref mut y) => … }` over a `Box` — and a row may not be bought
# with a legal-program refusal. Holding a demonstrated `&`-write hole open to
# satisfy a threshold meant for speculative work is the wrong trade; the
# threshold prices a GUESS, and this one was found by construction.
# ⚠ THE UNCONDITIONAL CALL WAS THE POINT. Asking a narrower `&`-only question
# here would have left two notions of writability in the tree, which is exactly
# how the gap opened. It costs a second correct refusal — `s.n += 1` on a
# non-`mut` local `s` — which is upstream E0594 and was zero on the corpus.
# 2026-08-29 — 361 -> 349. TWELVE rows, and the mechanism was priced as
# `callidxcallonly` in src/compiler/PROBES.md before a line of it was written.
# A place reached THROUGH A CALL THAT RETURNS A REFERENCE — a user `Deref` or
# `Index` impl, an autoref'd receiver — broke `extract_borrow_place`'s walk at
# the call, so `bp.root` stayed EMPTY and `record_borrow` returned on its first
# line: no loan at all, and the reader that would have refused was never asked.
# The fix hops to the receiver / arg0 and sets `index_in_chain`, which is the
# DEPOSIT half — `visit()`'s AddrOfTemp arm already records a whole-root borrow
# for an indexed chain, and a call is opaque exactly the way `[i]` is.
# PREDICTED TWELVE BY NAME; closed exactly those twelve, both diffs empty.
# ⚠ THE PROBE'S CEILING WAS THIRTEEN AND THE FIX CLOSES TWELVE, deliberately:
# the thirteenth, `borrowck-borrow-overloaded-auto-deref`, is `&mut p.y` on an
# `Rc<Point>` and rustc refuses it ONLY because Rust's `Rc` has no `DerefMut`.
# Logos's does (stdlib/lang/rc/rc.logos, "Rust parity" — it is not). Closing it
# through a borrow-check refusal would bank a row on a DIVERGENCE; its row
# stays, and its real fix is a stdlib question. Recorded so the next round does
# not re-derive it.
# ⚠ THREE SEMA REPAIRS WERE THE PRICE, all of them over-refusals of LEGAL Rust
# that the hop would otherwise have manufactured (a row may not be bought with a
# legal-program refusal):
#   · the FIELD auto-deref hardcoded `want_mut=false` — `&mut b.f` on a `Box`
#     crossed the shared `deref`. It now asks the use context, the question its
#     METHOD sibling already asks with `target_method_wants_mut_self`.
#   · a `match *b { … ref mut y … }` scrutinee did the same. The arms are in
#     hand where the scrutinee is lowered, so it was a question nobody asked.
#   · `emit_generic_deref_call`'s DerefMut fallback recovered the Target TYPE
#     and still emitted a `deref_mut` call that resolves to nothing — three
#     legal programs over a `Deref`-without-`DerefMut` struct died in mlir_gen
#     with "initializer produced no value ... statement DROPPED". A type
#     fallback is not a dispatch fallback.
# 2026-08-29 — 349 -> 343. SIX rows, and the mechanism was priced as
# `capprovnocap` in src/compiler/PROBES.md before a line of it was written:
# CEILING 6, corpus COST 0, over 11 420 arrivals at the site.
# `prov_of`'s ClosureCall arm has two permissive exits and the call's ARGUMENTS
# were read by NEITHER: `if (!caps) return {}` answered nothing for a
# CAPTURE-LESS closure — `note_closure_caps` ERASES the entry when the capture
# list is empty, so such a closure is indistinguishable there from a callee the
# walker cannot name — and the caps loop that follows merges captures only. The
# FnPtrCall branch three lines up already walked its args, so THE SAME PROGRAM
# refused through a fn, refused through a fn POINTER, and was admitted through a
# closure. The discriminator was a node kind, not a region.
# PREDICTED SIX BY NAME; closed exactly those six, both diffs empty.
# ⚠ WHAT LANDED IS NARROWER THAN THE PROBE (rule 7), and the probe is how that
# was found. `capprovnocap` merged EVERY reference argument into the result,
# which refuses this — legal, and in no corpus:
#     fn get(p:&i64)->&i64 { let l:i64=5;
#         let c=|x:&i64,y:&i64|->&i64{return y;}; return c(&l,p); }
# The result derives from `y` alone, so the tie to `&l` is a legal-program
# refusal and A ROW MAY NOT BE BOUGHT WITH ONE. What landed is the LANGUAGE'S
# OWN elision rule: with exactly ONE reference argument the result can only
# borrow THAT one and the answer is exact; with two or more there is no elision
# rule to apply — the tree refuses to let a FN even be WRITTEN in that shape
# (E0106, "more than one input lifetime and no `&self`") and a closure has no
# syntax to annotate the tie. All six rows pass exactly one reference, so the
# narrowing costs nothing here. The two-argument closure stays ADMITTED: it is
# a hole, it is named in PROBES.md as the group-A residue, and it has no
# imported row to sit on.
# ⚠ ONE OF THE SIX IS NOT CLASS C. `check-normalized-sig-for-wf` is nllmoves.A
# (`fn whoops<F>(s:&i64,f:F)->&'static i64 { return f(s); }`): the tie lands on
# a PARAM and the ELISION gate refuses it, not the dangling gate. A closure-call
# argument rule is a rule about calls, and it reaches outside the class that
# motivated it.
# 2026-08-29 — 343 -> 340. THREE rows, and the mechanism was priced as `fpsrc`
# in src/compiler/PROBES.md before a line of it was written: CEILING 3, corpus
# COST 0, over 2 213 384 arrivals at the site, and `fpboth` proved the ESCAPE
# channel (`prov_of`) adds neither a row nor a different row.
# THE MISSING OBSERVATION: A CLOSURE PARAMETER IS NOT A BORROW SOURCE. §B6 asks
# `collect_ref_sources_paths` what a value borrows; for a reference bound by a
# CLOSURE PARAMETER the answer was NOTHING, because a parameter is not a `let`
# and never went through `record_ref_sources`. So `x = y` inside a closure body,
# with `x` in the enclosing frame, deposited no source and `pop_scope` had
# nothing to find dying. That is E0521, "borrowed data escapes outside of
# closure", and it was three rows.
# PREDICTED THREE BY NAME (issue-45983, issue-7573, regions-escape-bound-fn-2);
# closed exactly those three, both diffs empty. All three name their local in
# the sentence — `y`, `installed`, `y` — because F5/F6's existing scope
# arithmetic is what spends the fact.
# ⚠ WHAT LANDED IS NARROWER THAN THE PROBE, TWICE (rule 7), and BOTH narrowings
# are legal programs the probe refused. `fpsrc` keyed on `closure_param_names_`,
# a set of STRINGS, and a set of strings cannot say which BINDING a name
# denotes:
#     give(|y| { let y: &i64 = &z; x = y; });        // legal, probe REFUSED it
# The shadow's borrow is of main's own `z`, which outlives every use of `x`.
# `names_live_closure_param` asks which FRAME declares the name instead, so the
# shadow disables the rule exactly where it IS the binding and not one frame
# further — pinned in both directions by the one-token pair
# pass/bc_f1_closure_param_shadow_legal and
# fail/bc_f1_closure_param_shadow_inner_block. The second narrowing: RECORDED
# SOURCES WIN, so a parameter reassigned in the body borrows what the assignment
# says and not itself.
# ⚠ FOUR OF THE SEVEN GROUP-F ROWS STAY ADMITTED, and each for a reason that is
# NOT this one — the group was three questions, not one (PROBES.md):
#   · regions-escape-bound-fn — the holder is NEVER READ again, so §B6 has no
#     use to report at. Needs a report AT THE WRITE (`fpwrite`, ceiling 4,
#     a whole new report site whose destination test is a context-level
#     stand-in for a region question). Deliberately not bought here.
#   · anonymous-region-in-apit--closure-param-escapes — the destination is the
#     ENCLOSING fn's own `&mut` PARAM, which note_holder_escape_prov skips by
#     #78/#138. Task #78, not a closure question.
#   · nll/escape-argument--t09 — `*q = r`, both ends parameters of ONE closure
#     dying in ONE frame. The only F row that genuinely needs a call-site write
#     summary.
#   · borrowck/borrowed-data-escapes-closure-148392 — a `move` body;
#     `walk_closure_body` returns at its first line.
# AND THE INFERENCE-DRIVEN RESIDUE IS NAMED: a closure with no trait bound
# (`let mut c = |y| { x = y; }; c(&z);`) stays admitted, because the gate is the
# parameter's TYPE. That is, by accident, exactly rustc's bound-driven vs
# inference-driven split — but it is an accident, not a proof, and it has no
# imported row to sit on.
# 2026-08-29 — 340 -> 337. THREE rows across THREE roots, and the mechanism was
# priced as `tmcbdyn` in src/compiler/PROBES.md: CEILING 3, corpus COST 0.
# THE MISSING OBSERVATION: AN ERASED PAYLOAD HIDES A BORROW. `Box<dyn Trait>`,
# `impl Trait` and a closure can HOLD a `&` their type never spells.
# `type_hides_borrow` — the return gate's sibling — has always listed those four
# kinds verbatim; `type_may_carry_borrow` never knew them, so an erased result
# opened no gate, tied to no receiver and merged no argument provenance.
# ⚠ TWO OF THE THREE CONTAIN NO CLOSURE AT ALL — they are `Box<dyn Trait>`
# holding a `&`. The closure rows are only the subset whose erased payload
# happens to be a closure.
# PREDICTED THREE BY NAME (do-not-suggest-adding-move-move, issue-55796--r09b,
# regions-close-param-into-object--b-object-dangles); closed exactly those
# three, both diffs empty.
# ⚠ WHAT LANDED IS NARROWER THAN THE PROBE, AND THE NARROWING IS RULE 5 (a COST
# of 0 is not a safety claim). `type_may_carry_borrow` has 28 consumers, counted
# by tools/dlog/tmcb_sites.dl and not by grep, and 10.87 MILLION arrivals. One
# hand-written counter-example PER CONSUMING SITE — 26 live sites, 26 programs,
# each proven to reach its site by an armed fire log — found the widening
# REFUSING TWO LEGAL PROGRAMS, at sites the corpus had priced COST 0:
#   · collect_ref_sources_paths' Call ENTRY — `fn mkb(n:&i64) -> Box<dyn Give>
#     { return Box::new(Sq{s:*n}); }` COPIES `*n`, and the widened entry gate
#     deposits the argument as a §B6 source anyway: E0597 on a legal program.
#   · prov_of's #86 sub-site C — a receiver of type `Cell<Box<dyn Give>>` whose
#     method returns its ARGUMENT and says so exactly; the result adopted the
#     RECEIVER's locality. That arm's own comment states this cannot happen —
#     true of the predicate as written, false the moment it learns erasure.
# So the knowledge is NOT applied to the predicate. It is applied at the FOUR
# ARMS that discharge rule 5, through a separately-named entry
# `type_may_carry_borrow_erased`: §B6's MethodCall by-flow gate, `prov_of`'s
# Call door, `check_return_value`'s holds_gate, and visit_stmt's #86 Let
# sub-site 2. Those four close the SAME THREE ROWS and admit all 36 hand
# programs, the two refusals included. Adding a fifth caller is a measurement,
# not an edit.
# ⚠ A CEILING IS NOT ADDITIVE OVER SITES (rule 6). Armed one site at a time,
# 3578 closes 1 and every other site closes 0 — sum 1, whole 3. Per row:
# issue-55796--r09b needs {MethodCall by_flow}; do-not-suggest-adding-move-move
# needs {holds_gate, Let sub-site 2}; regions-close-param-into-object needs
# {prov_of Call door, holds_gate}. Two of the three are invisible to any single
# site, and a per-site sweep reading only that column would have killed this
# mechanism as ceiling 1.
# RULE 7, AND IT BIT: regions-close-param-into-object printed "local variable
# '?'" — §B6 has no Call answer, because the widening that WOULD supply the name
# is the one that over-refuses. The name is recovered at the REPORT site, where
# only the string is read and the verdict is already made; it now says `local`.
# STILL ADMITTED, each for a reason that is not this one: escape-argument--t09
# (needs a call-site write summary), anonymous-region-in-apit--closure-param-
# escapes (task #78), borrowed-data-escapes-closure-148392 (a `move` body), and
# the 22 consuming sites of `type_may_carry_borrow` that no counter-example has
# yet discharged — `tmcbdyn` still arms all 28 and still refuses those two.
# 2026-08-29 — 337 -> 334. THREE rows, THREE ARMS, all priced at COST 0 and each
# a MISSING QUESTION rather than a new rule. The round's subject was the survey
# of GROUP B (`bck.B` 21 + `nllmoves.B` 13 = 34 rows, seventeen questions —
# src/compiler/PROBES.md, "GROUP B SURVEYED BY MISSING OBSERVATION"), and these
# are the three of the seventeen that are literally what the `B` label claims
# AND cost nothing.
#   · `fldrootbits` — `field_borrow_conflicts` read only the two PATH maps.
#     The VarState declaration has said in words since B83 that "(mut_borrowed,
#     shared_borrows) act as borrows on path "" — they conflict with every field
#     path"; the reader never implemented it, so `let b = &a;` (root bits) was
#     invisible to `let z = a.i;` (path question). 5 302 137 arrivals, CEILING 1.
#     ⚠ THE 2026-08-28 CLANG ENUMERATION CHECKED THE OTHER DIRECTION — a root
#     reader holding a path — and correctly found 0. Right answer, wrong way
#     round, and that is why 5.3M arrivals sat under a 0 for a day.
#     ⚠⚠ AND ONLY HALF OF IT LANDED — the fifth round running that the landing
#     is NARROWER than the thing that was priced, and this time MEASURED, not
#     argued. The probe had two branches; the `mut_borrowed` one buys ZERO rows
#     (ledger 334/334 green without it) and its entire effect on 1860 `bc` tests
#     was to REWORD TEN ALREADY-RED DIAGNOSTICS — the whole-var reader answers
#     that question at every site the corpus reaches. One question with two names
#     is the defect this file keeps recording, so only
#     `need_exclusive && shared_borrows > 0` — the half that buys the row — is in
#     the tree. The `mut_borrowed` half is a diagnostic-precision task, not a
#     checker one, and it is left open with its price named: 10 pinned texts.
#   · `recvfieldpath` — `check_recv_conflict` BAILED on any non-empty path, so a
#     `&mut self` method call whose receiver is a FIELD place asked no conflict
#     question at all, while the whole-var spelling was already refused. 91
#     arrivals, CEILING 1. A delegation to the maps that already hold the loans.
#   · `tupidxmove` — visit()'s `TupleIndex` arm was `visit_place_base(receiver);
#     break;` and nothing else. A tuple element is a FIELD whose name is its
#     index, already `extract_borrow_place`'s spelling, so `FieldRead`'s ~140
#     lines of bookkeeping now serve both through ONE segment walk. 8 arrivals,
#     CEILING 1. ⚠ RULE 4 DECLARED: eight fires is the ENTIRE population of
#     "a move-typed tuple projection in a consuming position" over this ledger
#     plus 1385 legal programs, so the ceiling bounds a count and nothing else.
#     What funds it is that the arm was MISSING, not the number.
# PREDICTED THREE BY NAME before the edit (borrowck-move-from-subpath-of-
# borrowed-path, issue-82032, move-out-of-tuple-field); closed exactly those
# three, both diffs empty over all 337.
# ⚠ COST 0 IS NOT A SAFETY CLAIM (rule 5), AND THE CORPUS CANNOT MAKE ONE
# (rule 10): both halves of the harness consist only of programs that COMPILE.
# 23 HAND-WRITTEN MULTI-LINE COUNTER-EXAMPLES, each proven to reach its arm by
# an armed fire log printing the place and the state it was asked about, all 23
# admitted: the NLL direction at each arm (a root loan dead before the field
# move / the field call), shared-vs-shared, disjoint siblings at all three
# granularities (field, tuple element, method receiver), a Copy tuple element
# read twice and read in a loop, `s.t.0` (a tuple inside a struct field), a
# tuple element as a method-call PLACE BASE, a read through `&(B,B)`, and the
# ARM1xARM2 COMPOSITE — `recvfieldpath` now delegates INTO `fldrootbits`, so a
# whole-root `&mut` and a field-place call meet, and ce20 proves that path is
